think Read crux/REMEMBER.md (Live edge + Git nib block + gates), the 1024-round itinerary, the eight-season double-seat, and the rye-first crypto wave for the road ahead. Confirmed the named Season G crux (image/color.rye sRGB algebra) already stands GREEN and whole, and crypto stands WHOLE -- neither is the open gap. The live thread is the long productive Season G open-media font run; the prior lap landed the Latin-1 acute/cedilla corpus and its own recommendation named the grave/circumflex vowels and the eszett as the next accented coverage. Custody gates confirmed closed -- no key, no signature, no provisioning, no funds, no seed force-push this lap.
think Chose the Lindy-first crux among the open accented gaps: the five grave-accent vowels (a/e/i/o/u grave). Higher Lindy than a decorative corpus because these letters let a civic right-to-repair device spell a French, Italian, or Portuguese keeper's OWN name -- Andrea, Nicole, Fabio, Antonio, Ilaria -- with the accent that name actually carries, the same durable argument the acute lap made for the Iberian hand. A coherent five-vowel set at strictly ascending non-contiguous codepoints U+00E0,U+00E8,U+00EC,U+00F2,U+00F9.
think Council rota by lap index: the prior lap deep-read item 22 (context/SILO_TECHNIQUE.md); cycling forward, this lap deep-read item 23 -- context/specs/20260627-102012_one-clock-naming-law.md. Fitting, since this lap mints a fresh one-clock stamp (20260817.055434): later is always larger, the name carries its own when, no number computed -- the law's own gesture.
think Designed the crux proof as the deliberate MIRROR of the acute placement proof. The acute mark in font5x7_latin1 rises to the right (top-row ink right-of-center); the grave mark here descends to the right and sits LEFT-of-center. So the honest, auditable invariant is symmetric: each grave vowel carries top-row ink in the left half (columns 0-1, band [0,center_col)) and NONE in the right half (columns 3-4, band [center_col+1,cell_w)). This is what separates a grave letter from its acute twin -- which way the mark leans.
think Reused the acute vowel bodies (rows 2-6) unchanged and swapped only the mark rows 0-1 to the grave stroke (row0 col1, row1 col2), so the corpus reads as a true sibling of the acute set rather than a fresh hand. Added a single half-open band reader row_band_ink(cov,r,c0,c1) with three positive invariants (ordered band, inside width, count bounded), and two named lookups top_left_ink_of / top_right_ink_of. The left-lean and right-empty checks are two separate named guards (GraveNotLeftLeaning / GraveMarkDriftedRight) from the start, so no compound assert was ever written.
think Hand-computed ink counts before running: a grave 16, e grave 16, i grave 10, o grave 14, u grave 14 -- band lo 10, hi 16, inside the honest [8,18]. Codepoints strictly ascending, count 5, last is u grave U+00F9.
obs GREEN on the first metal run, no red: ink band 10..16 exactly as hand-computed, encoded 247 bytes over raw 175, all five present/distinct/legible, every mark proven leaning left. tame_style_check bans clean, width-check exit 0, both files plain 7-bit ASCII though the glyphs cover a non-ASCII range (the ascii-first font-codec exception).
obs Pure additive font content -- a fresh module and witness; the acute corpus and every sibling stand untouched. No network, no key, no funds; no custody gate reached. Latin-1 accented coverage now carries the acute/cedilla set and the grave set; the circumflex vowels and the German eszett remain the next agent-doable rungs to complete it.
